ess very well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), makes nasty remarks (professional communication), can be uncooperative (crew cooperation), grumpy (attitude). Strengths: reliability, stress, technical. Weaknesses: memory, cooperation, communication.\n\nCandidate D: takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), solves problems extremely well (technical competence, decision quality), concentrates very well (attention), responds adequately to unexpected events (stress resilience moderate), hot temper (crew cooperation), regarded as arrogant/know-it-all (crew cooperation), relatively weak leadership (crew cooperation), moody, loner (crew cooperation). Strengths: responsibility, problem solving, concentration. Weaknesses: temperament, arrogance, weak leadership.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate is overall better. Both have serious crew cooperation concerns. B's uncooperative and nasty remarks are direct threats. D's hot temper and arrogance also threats. B's weak memory for numbers is a safety-critical issue for attention and accuracy. D's concentration is strong, no memory issue noted. D's stress response is \"adequate\", not as strong as B's \"handles stress very well\". However, D's responsibility and problem solving may be more critical for safety. Which is more severe? The panel has been split. As Agent 2, we can either maintain B or shift to D. We need to provide a focused argumentative contribution referencing prior discussion.\n\nGiven the evidence, I think B's weak memory for numbers is a concrete safety risk (e.g., fuel calculations). D's temperament may be mitigated. So I might shift to D? But earlier we argued B's stress handling is stronger. However, the panel has been leaning D (Agent 1 and 3). To converge, maybe we should shift to D. But we must be consistent with our own reasoning. The guidelines say we can change vote if evidence supports.